# Ubuntu Core Overview
Quick reference for Ubuntu Core architecture and key concepts.

## What is Ubuntu Core?
Ubuntu Core is a minimal, immutable OS for IoT and embedded devices. It is
built entirely from snaps — every component, including the kernel, gadget,
and base, is a snap. This makes it fully transactional and OTA-updateable.

## Core snap types

**kernel**    The Linux kernel and drivers. Updated as a snap.
**gadget**    Board-specific configuration: partition layout, bootloader
              settings, and gadget.yaml. Unique to each hardware platform.
**base**      The root filesystem snap (e.g. core24, core22). Provides libc
              and standard utilities.
**app**       Regular application snaps. Strictly confined by default.
**snapd**     The snap daemon itself. Updated as a snap on Core.

## Partition layout (typical)
ubuntu-seed    FAT32   Read-only seed partition. Holds model assertion,
                       system label, and seeded snap files.
ubuntu-boot    ext4    Boot assets and recovery system data.
ubuntu-save    ext4    Persistent data that survives factory reset.
ubuntu-data    ext4    Main data partition: /home, snap data, state.json.
               (LUKS-encrypted on full disk encryption images)

## First boot sequence
1. Bootloader loads the kernel from ubuntu-boot.
2. snapd (in initramfs mode) reads the model assertion from ubuntu-seed.
3. Seeded snaps from ubuntu-seed are installed to ubuntu-data.
4. snapd writes state.json and transitions to "run" mode.
5. The gadget snap configures the device (interfaces, serial, etc.)
6. modeenv records mode=run when first boot completes successfully.

## Key files on a running device
/var/lib/snapd/state.json      snapd state: installed snaps, changes, tasks
/var/lib/snapd/modeenv         Current boot mode (run/recover/install)
/var/lib/snapd/device/        Device identity and serial assertion
/var/lib/snapd/snaps/          Mounted .snap files
/var/lib/snapd/mount/          Mount namespace symlinks

## Useful commands on device
snap list                      Installed snaps
snap changes                   Recent snap operations
snap connections <snap>        Interface connections for a snap
snap logs <snap> -n 50         Last 50 log lines
sudo snap install --dangerous  Install unreviewed/unsigned snap

